From 91dd9235eda4c053e970d166667594229023c7d1 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dag Sommer <dagaleks@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 13 Aug 2026 17:33:41 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 28/37] windows: load an external ELF kernel instead of
 refusing

`krun_start_enter` on Windows failed with -EINVAL and
KernelLoaderUnavailable("loading an external kernel image") before a WHP
partition was ever created: build_microvm calls load_payload before
setup_vm, and Payload::ExternalKernel was a stub.

That stub was not a corner case. `krun_set_kernel` is the only way a
caller supplies a kernel to a Windows build -- there is no libkrunfw DLL
in this configuration -- and on x86_64 it produces Payload::KernelCopy
only for format 0 (Raw), which krun_set_kernel maps as a bundled kernel.
Format 1 (Elf), which is what containerd's nerdbox passes on x86_64,
produces Payload::ExternalKernel. So the only load path Windows
supported and the only one its callers use were disjoint.

Route the ELF format through kernel::loader::elf. Everything the 64-bit
boot protocol needs already exists on this path and is unchanged: the
command line is written to CMDLINE_START by load_cmdline, the zero page
by arch::x86_64::configure_64bit_boot with cmd_line_ptr, the e820 map
and any ramdisk, and regs::setup_regs enters the guest in long mode at
e_entry with RSI pointing at the zero page. Only the segment copy and
the entry address were missing.

pvh is reported as false unconditionally. arch::x86_64::configure_pvh is
Linux-only, so a `true` would make configure_system skip *all* boot
configuration and hand the guest an unwritten zero page; and setup_regs
on WHP always enters in long mode, which is not what a PVH entry point
expects. A kernel carrying the PVH note still boots, through startup_64
like a kernel without one -- it just does not take the same path Linux
would take for that image.

The other formats stay refused, now with KernelFormatUnsupported.
KernelFormat::Raw cannot reach here on x86_64, and the three compressed
Image variants decompress to ELF and could use the same loader, but
nothing on Windows produces them and an untested decompression path is
worse than a clear refusal.

The initramfs load, which is identical on both paths, moves into a
shared helper.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
